  "id": "Compound::DB00892 - Gene::6336",
  "verbalization": "Oxybuprocaine, Chloroprocaine, and Procaine are all substituted anilines that share a common structural motif of a benzoate ester group, which is a key feature in their pharmacological profile. These compounds are known to interact with ion channels, particularly the Scn10a channel, which is involved in the regulation of neuronal excitability. The evidence suggests that Oxybuprocaine, through its structural similarity to other procaine derivatives, binds to Scn10a, potentially modulating its function. The recurring mechanism of interaction involves the benzoate ester group, which is a common structural feature among these compounds, and the binding to Scn10a is supported by the observed interactions in multiple paths.\n\nThe evidence from the paths is consistent with a mechanism of action involving the binding of substituted anilines to Scn10a, with the benzoate ester group playing a critical role in this interaction. The low common ancestors across the paths indicate a shared structural and functional relationship among the compounds, reinforcing the hypothesis that Oxybuprocaine may act on Scn10a through a similar mechanism as other procaine derivatives. The presence of anesthetic agents and agents affecting the nervous system in the paths further supports the notion that these compounds are involved in neurochemical processes, which aligns with the known role of Scn10a in neuronal function. The integrated evidence suggests a plausible mechanism of action involving the binding of substituted anilines to Scn10a, with the benzoate ester group serving as a critical structural element in this interaction.",
